      "content": "The strength of the \"trans women are girls\" argument lies not in denying biological differences but in emphasizing that the current sports landscape already institutionalizes identity-based category changes through IAAF sanctions, meaning the regulatory framework already accepts gender identity when defined by sex reassignment. Biologically, while pre-transition differences exist, the exclusion logic often fails to account for how category selection functions in other sports like wrestling and soccer, where aging or height thresholds similarly exclude individuals from competing in categories where they would be numerically superior. Policy prioritization becomes subjective because no legislation can fully predict the interplay of adolescent growthons to, hormonal fluctuations, and lagging tissue maturation versus high-intensity training adaptations, leading to valid debates on how to balance empirical data with adaptive rule-making. Ultimately, the most robust policies are not based on a single physiological fact but on a holistic application of the amateur spirit alongside medical reality to allow athletes to compete on fair ground.",
